// === PREAMBLE ===
// @trope    Taking You With Me
// @category Combat / Death / Sacrifice
// @source   https://allthetropes.org/wiki/Taking_You_with_Me
// @domain   Interpersonal
// @version  1.3
//
// Laconic (per the wiki): someone who knows they are already dead spends that death to make sure
// their enemy dies too — the last act of a doomed combatant is the mutual kill. FRONTIER: the
// MUTUAL-DESTRUCTION read of the action-dynamics Finisher. Cousin to Heroic Sacrifice — but the
// inverted aim is the whole point. Where the Sacrifice rule spends the giver's position so an ALLY
// is [+Saved] (the giver [+Fallen]), Taking You With Me spends a [+Doomed] combatant's certain
// death so the ENEMY is [+Fallen] too: the one final deed lands both bodies. Nothing is saved; what
// is bought is the foe's destruction. Where Defiant to the End spends the death to buy NOTHING (bare
// dignity), this spends it to buy the enemy's grave — one blow, two corpses.


// === IMPORTS & ASSOCIATIONS ===
import "trl/concepts/index.trl"            // conflict, death, revenge
import "trl/modules/action_dynamics.trl"   // Finishes -> the decisive final blow; [+Fallen]

// Lateral thematic links (optional, machine-followable — for discovery / embeddings).
assoc taking_you_with_me -> [
  action_dynamics  : "the mutual-kill read of the Finisher of",
  heroic_sacrifice : "the inverted aim of — that spends the death to SAVE an ally; this to DESTROY a foe",
  defiant_to_the_end : "the death buys NOTHING there; here it buys the enemy's grave",
  death            : "is spent — the agent's own, certain — to deal another",
  revenge          : "the doomed combatant's last is often a final stroke of"
]


// === ONTOLOGY ===
// A doomed combatant is one whose own death is already certain (the body is forfeit). To Take You
// With Me is to spend that forfeit death as the instrument of the enemy's: the one final deed leaves
// BOTH [+Fallen]. It is the mutual-destruction face of the Finisher — not a guard, not a gift, but a
// trade of one certain death for another. The deed is its own grave-marker for two.
imply MutualKill        -> [Finisher, Reciprocal, Conflict]
imply TakingYouWithMe   -> [MutualKill, Doomed, Final]


// === ABSTRACT RULE ===
// Recognition: a [+Doomed] combatant ($a) — own death already certain — lands a final, decisive
// strike (the action-dynamics Finishes verb) on an enemy ($y). The signature that distinguishes this
// from a plain Finisher is that the AGENT is already doomed: the deed is not a victory but a mutual
// kill — the agent spends the death that is already coming as the instrument of the foe's. So the
// one final deed leaves both bodies down — the enemy [+Fallen] by the blow, and the doomed agent
// [+Fallen] by the death that was already certain. The deed is tagged [+TakingYouWithMe]; the agent
// touches the death concept they spend, and the revenge the last stroke so often carries. (The
// symmetric `><` of the mutual fight is carried as story-graph flavor in the vignette below.)
rule Taking_You_With_Me {
  when:
    char $a [+Doomed]
    evt $f [&Finishes(agent=$a, target=$y)]
  then:
    // the one final deed — the enemy falls by the blow
    $y [+Fallen]
    // and the doomed agent falls with them: the death that was already certain
    $a [+Fallen]
    // the mutual kill is named on the deed
    $f [+TakingYouWithMe]
    $a -- death : "Spends To Deal"
    $a -- revenge : "Last Stroke Of"
}


// === CONCRETE VIGNETTE: Star Trek II: The Wrath of Khan — the Genesis gambit (film, 1982) ===
// Beaten in the nebula, his ship a hulk and his body broken, Khan Noonien Singh is a dead man and
// knows it. He will not let Kirk walk away. With his dying strength he arms the Genesis Device —
// "From hell's heart I stab at thee; for hate's sake I spit my last breath at thee" — to detonate
// and take the Enterprise with him into the grave. The one deed is meant to land both crews; Kirk
// slips the blast by minutes, but Khan dies having spent his certain death as a weapon — the
// doomed combatant's mutual kill, aimed true even if it falls short.

// KHAN NOONIEN SINGH — the broken superman, dying, set on one last blow
char khan [+Antagonist] [+Doomed]
khan [~Committed_To(target=revenge)]

// JAMES T. KIRK / THE ENTERPRISE — the enemy he means to take with him
char kirk [+Protagonist]

// THE GENESIS DEVICE — the lifegiving torpedo turned into a grave for two ships
obj genesis_device [+MacGuffin] [+Doomsday]

// INT. RELIANT BRIDGE — DERELICT, NEBULA-DARK
set reliant_bridge [+Interior]

scene from_hells_heart {
  beat 1 {
    khan @ reliant_bridge
    // the duel through the nebula has come to its end — both ships crippled, the hate mutual
    khan >< kirk : "Mutual"
    // his own death is already certain; the body is forfeit
    khan [+Doomed]
  }
  beat 2 {
    khan > genesis_device
    // "From hell's heart I stab at thee" — he spends the death he cannot escape as the weapon
    evt arm_genesis [&Finishes(agent=khan, target=kirk)]
        dialog(inject=[speaker=khan], inline="""
               speaker: "{speaker}"
               cue: "dying at the dark console, hand crawling to the activation switch"
               tone: hateful, exultant, spent
               text: "From hell's heart I stab at thee; for hate's sake I spit my last breath at thee."
               """)
    // Taking_You_With_Me ([+Doomed] + Finishes on the mutual foe): the deed is the mutual kill
    kirk [+Fallen]
    khan [+Fallen]
    arm_genesis [+TakingYouWithMe]
    khan -- death : "Spends To Deal"
    khan -- revenge : "Last Stroke Of"
  }
}
